--TEST--
`Core\Cli::displayWidth` measures the string the sink will write and not the one it was handed, so a control byte costs the column its Control Picture costs and an SGR sequence cannot make a string measure short — `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink` and `rule:tooling/the-terminal-profile-resolves-once`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1's substitution is uniform and runs on the way to the terminal, so the
// bytes a program hands `echo` are never the bytes the screen shows. The
// measurement is taken on the second of those, which is what makes it safe to
// lay out against: a value that carried an escape sequence would otherwise
// measure zero columns for it and then draw five, and every box drawn around
// that value would be one that untrusted input chose the size of.
string $esc = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27);

echo "esc=", Core\Cli::displayWidth($esc), "\n";
echo "del=", Core\Cli::displayWidth(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(127)), "\n";
echo "c1=", Core\Cli::displayWidth(Core\Str::fromCodePoint(155)), "\n";

// The sequence is five columns because five pictures are what gets drawn —
// not zero, which is what it would cost a terminal that parsed it, and not
// one, which is what a member counting "a control byte is invisible" would
// answer.
string $sgr = $esc . "[31m";
echo "sgr=", Core\Cli::displayWidth($sgr), "\n";

// The agreement that keeps the two halves of § 1 from drifting: `escape` is
// the same table reached as a value, so measuring before it and measuring
// after it are the same number, and the escaped text is what both were about.
echo "escaped=", Core\Cli::displayWidth(Core\Cli::escape($sgr)), "\n";
echo "shown=", Core\Cli::escape($sgr), "\n";
--EXPECT--
esc=1
del=1
c1=1
sgr=5
escaped=5
shown=␛[31m
